#!/bin/sh
# ============================================================
# getty service (serial console) — run script
# s6 keeps this supervised. If it dies, s6 restarts it.
#
# The local VGA console (tty1, see ../getty-tty1) needs the
# virtio-gpu KMS driver loaded to actually display anything --
# it's built as a module and nothing loads it by default, so
# nothing ever renders it visible in a headless QEMU run (or any
# host with no framebuffer console backing tty1). ttyS0 is what the
# kernel cmdline (console=ttyS0,115200n8) and scripts/mkiso.sh's
# GRUB config already treat as the primary console for headless
# boot verification (scripts/mkvm.sh --display none), so give it
# its own getty rather than relying on a console nothing can see.
#
# down-signal / timeout-down are load-bearing, not tuning.
#
# getty execs login, which execs the shell, so the process s6
# supervises IS the interactive shell -- and an interactive shell
# ignores SIGTERM by definition. With timeout-down unset (which means
# wait forever), `s6-rc -bDa change` sat on "service getty-ttyS0:
# stopping" and never returned; s6-linux-init-shutdownd waits for that
# script with a plain wait_pid() and no timeout of its own, so it never
# reached the stage that SIGTERMs everything, SIGKILLs the remainder
# and calls reboot(2). The machine could not shut down at all while
# anyone was logged in -- which is always, since somebody has to type
# `poweroff`.
#
# SIGHUP rather than a bigger hammer: a getty going away IS a hangup,
# and that is the signal a shell is specified to die on. timeout-down
# is the backstop for anything that ignores even that -- 4 seconds,
# then s6-rc gives up, rc.shutdown exits non-zero, shutdownd warns and
# carries on to the sweep that ends the machine. Losing a shell rudely
# at that point is fine. Hanging forever was not.
#
# ── TERM, AND WHY IT IS AN ARGUMENT HERE ─────────────────────────────
#
# busybox getty takes `BAUD_RATE TTY [TERMTYPE]` and puts TERMTYPE in
# the environment for login and the shell below it. Without it TERM is
# EMPTY -- measured on a booted machine, `echo $TERM` printed nothing
# for the whole life of this project.
#
# WHAT THAT COSTS WAS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, and the first version of
# this comment got it wrong: it said an empty TERM leaves the Python
# REPL printing ^[[A for the up-arrow. It does not. readline's arrow
# keys are COMPILED-IN bindings, not terminfo-derived, so history
# recall survives an empty TERM (checked both ways on a booted
# machine). What does not survive is `curses`:
# `curses.setupterm()` fails outright with "could not find terminfo
# database", so half of what RFC 0026 roadmap 2 is for cannot work at
# all -- and readline gets no cursor capabilities either
# (`tigetstr("cuu1")` has nothing to return), which is redisplay on a
# resize and multi-line editing rather than basic recall.
#
# The name has to be one ncurses can resolve. build/40-ncurses.sh ships
# no terminfo database and compiles a named set into the library
# instead; `vt100` is on that list. Changing either without the other
# gives a terminal description that cannot be found.
#
# vt100, because a serial line does not say what is on the other end of
# it, and vt100 is the description every real terminal implements.
# ============================================================
exec s6-setuidgid root /sbin/getty 115200 ttyS0 vt100
